      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The best treatment for cryoglobulinemic syndrome (CS) is still an unsolved problem. Recently colchicine has been successfully used to treat vasculitides and other immune-mediated diseases. Therefore, we undertook to treat 17 CS patients with colchicine (1 mg/day for 6-48 months), 8 of them with essential mixed cryoglobulinemia (EMC) and 9 with CS secondary to liver disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "In all patients the clinical and laboratory features were evaluated at the beginning of the study and during the first 6-12 months; 10 cases were followed for a longer period (18-48 months)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During the first period symptoms improved as follows: purpura in 15 of 17 patients, weakness in 9 of 14 and leg ulcers in 3 of 5. Hepatic and renal function tests, hypocomplementemia, rheumatoid factor (RF) titres and the cryocrit also improved. Prolonged follow-up showed a relapse in the different variables, although they remained at better levels than at the beginning. Only the cryocrit showed a further reduction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Though this was a preliminary open study it shows that colchicine is an efficient treatment in CS and suggests that a controlled clinical study should be performed to assess its real value."
        }
      ]},
